--- !u!114 &1756796844
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1756796843}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f78d5c848fb4fdd41a9c77d4972b4ceb, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  triviaPanel: {fileID: 2139086522}
  factText: {fileID: 12189723}
  funFacts:
  - Did you know... The Marbled Water Monitor (Varanus marmoratus) is a master of
    disguise? Found in Luzon and nearby islands, its skin is covered in intricate,
    pale yellow spots that look like marbled stone, helping it vanish into the riverbanks.
    They are excellent swimmers and can stay underwater for up to 30 minutes!
  - Did you know... The Asian Palm Civet (Paradoxurus hermaphroditus) is the "coffee
    connoisseur" of the forest? Locally known as Musang or Alamid, it eats the ripest
    coffee cherries. The beans pass through its system and are collected to make
    Kopi Luwak, one of the most expensive and rarest coffees in the world.
  - Did you know... The Batanes Reed Warbler (Iduna baticata) is a true islander?
    This tiny, melodic songbird lives exclusively on the northernmost islands of
    the Philippines, like Batan and Itbayat. It thrives in the windy grasslands and
    thickets where other birds might struggle to survive.
  - Did you know... The Philippine Serpent-Eagle (Spilornis holospilus) is a specialized
    hunter with "armor" on its legs? To protect itself from the venomous bites of
    the snakes it hunts, it has thick, scaly skin on its feet and legs that acts
    like a shield while it swoops through the forest canopy.
  - Did you know... The Lowland Stripe-backed Shrew Rat (Chrotomys mindorensis) is
    not your typical "pest" rat? This unique rodent is actually a specialized eater
    that ignores cheese and grain in favor of earthworms! It uses its long snout
    and sturdy claws to dig through the damp soil of Mindoro and Luzon.
  - "Did you know... The Palawan Flying Fox (Acerodon leucotis) is like a giant gardener
    with wings? Despite its \"fox\" name, it\u2019s a fruit bat that can have a wingspan
    of nearly four feet! By flying long distances to eat fruit, it spreads seeds
    across the islands, helping to regrow the Palawan rainforests."
  - Did you know... The Bicol Narrow-mouthed Frog (Kaloula kalingensis) is a tiny
    "balloon" of the forest? During the rainy season, these small frogs inflate their
    bodies to look bigger and let out a loud, booming call that sounds much larger
    than they actually are. They are found only in the lush southern tip of Luzon.
  - Did you know... The Aspin gets its name from "Asong Pinoy," which simply means
    Filipino Dog? They are a special mix of breeds known for being incredibly smart,
    loyal, and naturally healthy!
  - Did you know... The Manta Ray is the "brainiac" of the fish world? It has the
    largest brain-to-body ratio of any fish and shows signs of self-awareness, like
    recognizing its own reflection. Unlike stingrays, they have no venomous barb;
    they use their massive wings to glide like underwater birds while filtering tiny
    plankton.
  - Did you know... The Dolphin is the ocean's ultimate acrobat? Certain species,
    like the Spinner Dolphin, can leap and rotate their bodies up to seven times
    in one jump. This isn't just for show; the spinning helps them shake off parasites
    and communicate with their pod through the loud, echoing splashes they create.
  - Did you know... The Eel has a "hidden" weapon straight out of a movie? Because
    Moray Eels live in tight crevices and can't easily suck in food, they evolved
    a second set of jaws in their throat. While the front teeth grip the prey, these
    inner jaws spring forward, grab the meal, and pull it down into the stomach.
  - "Did you know... The Sea Turtle is the ultimate \"time traveler\"? Using an internal
    GPS that senses the Earth\u2019s magnetic field, they navigate thousands of miles
    across the open sea. Remarkably, after decades of traveling, a female will return
    to the exact same beach where she was born to lay her own eggs."
  - "Did you know... The Whale Shark is a gentle giant with a \"starry\" secret?
    Each shark has a unique pattern of white spots that acts like a human fingerprint,
    allowing scientists to identify them individually. Despite being the largest
    fish in the ocean\u2014growing as long as a large bus\u2014they have no interest
    in hunting big prey and instead use 3,000 tiny teeth to filter-feed on microscopic
    plankton. "
  - Did you know... The Mindanao Gymnure is often called a "hairy hedgehog," but
    it has a very different way of protecting itself? Instead of sharp quills, this
    small mammal found on the mountains of Mindanao has a distinct, strong odor that
    smells like rotten garlic or ammonia to ward off predators. It spends its nights
    scurrying through the damp forest floor hunting for insects and earthworms.
  - "Did you know... The Philippine Flying Lemur, or Kagwang, is a bit of a mystery\u2014it\u2019s
    not actually a lemur, and it can\u2019t technically fly? It is a \"glider\" with
    a massive flap of skin that stretches from its neck to its tail, acting like
    a built-in parachute. This allows it to soar gracefully between trees for over
    100 meters to find the tastiest young leaves in the rainforest canopy."
  - Did you know... The Catfish is essentially a swimming tongue? Their entire bodies,
    including their long "whiskers" or barbels, are covered in thousands of tiny
    taste buds. This allows them to "taste" their surroundings and find food in muddy
    or dark waters without ever needing to see it. Some species in the Philippines
    can even breathe air or use their fins to "walk" across land to find new ponds!
  - "Did you know... The Oriental Darter is known as the \"Snakebird\" for its incredible
    hunting style? It has a long, slender neck that it keeps kinked like a spring;
    when it sees a fish, it strikes with lightning speed, skewering the prey with
    its sharp, needle-like beak. Unlike other water birds, its feathers aren't waterproof,
    which helps it sink deeper underwater to hunt\u2014though it must spread its
    wings to dry in the sun afterward."
  - Did you know... The Tawi-Tawi Brown Dove is one of the rarest "ghosts" of the
    forest? This shy bird lives only on a few tiny islands in the Sulu Archipelago
    and spends most of its time on the forest floor rather than in the trees. Because
    it is so rare and lives in such a small, specific area, seeing one in the wild
    is a dream for birdwatchers and conservationists around the world.
  debugMode: 1
